SH(1)

NAME
     sh - the AZ/UX command interpreter

SYNOPSIS
     sh [-eilxfu] [-c command [arg0 [arg...]]]
     sh [-eilxfu] script [arg...]

DESCRIPTION
     sh is a POSIX-style shell. It reads commands from a terminal (with
     line editing), from a script or from -c. A login shell (argv[0]
     starting with '-', or -l) first reads /etc/profile and ~/.profile.

   Options
     -c  run the command string        -e  exit when a command fails
     -i  force interactive mode        -l  login shell
     -x  print commands as they run    -f  disable globbing
     -u  treat unset variables as errors (accepted)

   Commands
     A simple command is words and redirections:
         grep -i error log.txt > found 2>&1
     A pipeline connects commands: cmd1 | cmd2 | cmd3
     Lists: a; b (sequence)   a & (background)   a && b   a || b
     ! pipeline negates the status.

   Compound commands
     ( list )                       run in a subshell
     { list; }                      group
     if list; then list; [elif list; then list;]... [else list;] fi
     while list; do list; done      until list; do list; done
     for name [in word...]; do list; done
     case word in [(]pattern[|pattern]...) list;; ... esac
     name() compound-command        define a function

   Redirections
     < file   > file   >> file   >| file   <> file
     n>&m   n<&m   n>&-   <<WORD (here-document)   <<-WORD (strip tabs)
     A here-document delimiter in quotes suppresses expansion.

   Expansions
     ~ ~user                 home directories
     $name ${name}           variables; $1..$9 ${10} positional
     $# $@ $* $? $$ $! $- $0 special parameters
     ${n:-w} ${n:=w} ${n:+w} ${n:?w}   defaults and checks
     ${#n}  ${n#p} ${n##p} ${n%p} ${n%%p}   length, prefix/suffix removal
     $(cmd)  `cmd`           command substitution
     $((expr))               arithmetic (C operators, 64-bit)
     * ? [abc] [a-z] [!x]    file name patterns
     Quoting: '...' is literal; "..." allows $ and `; \ escapes one
     character.

   Built-in commands
     : true false . source alias bg break cd command continue echo eval
     exec exit export fg help history jobs kill local pwd read readonly
     return set shift test [ times trap type umask unalias unset wait

   Job control
     In an interactive shell each pipeline is a job. ^Z stops the
     foreground job; fg and bg continue it; jobs lists jobs; kill %n
     signals one. ^C interrupts the foreground job.

   Line editing
     Left/Right, ^A ^E (start/end), ^B ^F, Up/Down or ^P ^N (history),
     ^K ^U ^W (kill to end/start/word), ^Y (yank), ^L (clear), Tab
     (complete commands, file names and $variables; Tab twice lists).
     History is kept in ~/.sh_history.

   Variables used by the shell
     PATH HOME PS1 PS2 IFS PWD OLDPWD ENV. PS1 understands \u \h \w \W
     \$ \n \e.

EXIT STATUS
     The status of the last command; 127 if a command was not found,
     126 if it could not be run, 128+n if killed by signal n.

SEE ALSO
     intro(1), test(1), expr(1)
